# CODEOWNERS — automatic review assignment for path-sensitive files.
#
# Format: <path-pattern> <@user-or-team> [<@user-or-team> ...]
#
# Compliance artifacts are registry-level contracts that the
# `build_analysis` composition node enforces at graph-execution time.
# A drift here is a SOX §404 issue (the production audit trail no
# longer reflects what the compliance team classified), not an
# engineering one — so changes REQUIRE a compliance-team reviewer on
# every PR.
#
# See: compliance/audit_bearing_registry.md (workshop minutes,
# 2026-04-22) and prompts/pre_code_session.md §"WORKING-STYLE
# CONSTRAINTS".
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reviewer resolution (2026-05-09)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The ADR-038 §8.5 five-layer map below was authored with team-handle
# placeholders (`@compliance-team`, `@backend-team`, `@frontend-team`,
# `@architect`, `@platform-team`, `@sre-team`, `@domain-sme`,
# `@tools-admin`). At present the repo has two human contributors
# besides Claude:
#
#   * @kumarabhijit  — primary maintainer / Architect / Compliance lens
#   * @linkinrustle  — secondary maintainer
#
# Every placeholder is therefore resolved to both handles so review
# load is shared and the file actually enforces assignment. The
# layered structure is preserved verbatim in the comments so the
# reviewer-mapping can re-fan-out when GitHub teams are created.
# When that happens, reverse the resolution by replacing
# `@kumarabhijit @linkinrustle` with the appropriate `@org/team`
# handle per layer; the path patterns stay unchanged.

